<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piet Mondrian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Piet Mondriaan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piet Mondrian was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter and a pioneer of abstract art. With Theo van Doesburg he founded the De Stijl movement in 1917, and he developed Neo-Plasticism, painting grids of black lines with blocks of primary colours on white. His work strongly influenced modern design and architecture. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and from 1940 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-mondrian.jpg" alt="Piet Mondrian" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piet_Mondriaan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pieter Cornelis Mondriaan was born on 7 March 1872 in Amersfoort, the second of his parents' children. His father was a qualified drawing teacher. With his uncle Frits Mondriaan, a pupil of Willem Maris of the Hague School, the young Piet often painted and drew along the river Gein. After a strict Protestant upbringing he entered the Academy for Fine Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1892, already qualified as a teacher.</p><p>His early work is naturalistic or Impressionistic, mostly landscapes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a>, fields and rivers, and it shows the influence of pointillism and the vivid colours of Fauvism. The painting Evening (Avond) of 1908 uses a palette of almost entirely red, yellow and blue and is the earliest of his paintings to emphasise primary colours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mondrian moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1912 and dropped an a from his surname to mark his break with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The Cubism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a> and Georges Braque appeared at once in his work, but he saw it as a port of call rather than a destination. Staying in the Netherlands when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began in 1914, he met Bart van der Leck and Theo van Doesburg at the Laren artists' colony, and van der Leck's use of only primary colours influenced him. With Van Doesburg he founded the journal De Stijl, in which he published essays defining his theory of neoplasticism.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1918 and began grid-based paintings in late 1919. The lines in the early works are thin and grey, and in late 1920 and 1921 thick black lines separate larger and fewer forms, with more of them left white. In September 1938 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the face of advancing fascism, and in 1940, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was invaded and Paris fell, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. His late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> canvases have more lines than any of his work since the 1920s, in an overlapping arrangement that looks almost like a map.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Mondrian's art was utopian and sought universal values. He proclaimed in 1914 that art is higher than reality and has no direct relation to it. In 1913 he began to fuse his art with his theosophical studies, which marked his final break from representational painting. He limited his vocabulary to the three primary colours, red, blue and yellow, the three primary values of black, white and grey, and the two primary directions, horizontal and vertical. He died on 1 February 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piet Mondrian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch water engineering, social policies such as the first legal same-sex marriages in 2001, and artists from Vincent van Gogh to <b>Piet Mondrian</b> have had worldwide influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Painters rejected traditional perspective and took colour and shape, not the depiction of nature, as the essence of art; Wassily Kandinsky, <b>Piet Mondrian</b> and Kazimir Malevich sought to redefine art as the arrangement of pure colour.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Amersfoort, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.16,5.39&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Amersfoort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4671 362 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Piet Mondrian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5121 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5121 632h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.16,5.39&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
